                <pb facs="0196" n="104" sameAs="frus:frus1899/pg104"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr. Bridgman.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-01-24"><hi rendition="#italic">January 24,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 63.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    No. 73, of the 15th ultimo, reporting the outbreak of a revolution at La Paz,
                    and stating that it is probable you will be asked to shelter refugees.</p>
                <p>As to the question of shelter, as distinguished from so-called asylum, you may
                    consult the Deparment’s instruction to the minister of the United States to
                    Ecuador, printed in <ref target="frus:frus1895p1/pg_215">Foreign Relations,
                        1895, page 215</ref>.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
